      "text": "To find the \\( x \\)-intercept of the graph of the parabola given by the equation \\( x = 2y^2 - 6y + 3 \\), we need to set \\( y = 0 \\) and solve for \\( x \\).\n\n\\[\nx = 2(0)^2 - 6(0) + 3\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\( x \\)-intercept \\((a, 0)\\) is \\((3, 0)\\). Therefore, \\( a = 3 \\).\n\nFor the \\( y \\)-intercepts, we need to set \\( x = 0 \\) and solve for \\( y \\).\n\n\\[\n0 = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we solve using the quadratic formula \\( y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 2 \\), \\( b = -6 \\), and \\( c = 3 \\).\n\nCalculating the discriminant:\n\n\\[\nb^2 - 4ac = (-6)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 = 36 - 24 = 12\n\\]\n\nApplying the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{2 \\cdot 2}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the two \\( y \\)-intercepts are \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\). Therefore, \\( b = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( c = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum \\( a + b + c \\):\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\left( \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nCombining the fractions:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3} + 3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{6}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + 3\n\\]\n\\[\na + b + c = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
