      "text": "Given the equation of the parabola \\(x = 2y^2 - 6y + 3\\), we need to find the \\(x\\)-intercept, \\(y\\)-intercepts, and then calculate \\(a + b + c\\).\n\nFirst, find the \\(x\\)-intercept. The \\(x\\)-intercept occurs when \\(y = 0\\). Substituting \\(y = 0\\) into the equation gives:\n\\[\nx = 2(0)^2 - 6(0) + 3 = 3.\n\\]\nThus, the \\(x\\)-intercept is \\((3, 0)\\). So, \\(a = 3\\).\n\nNext, find the \\(y\\)-intercepts. The \\(y\\)-intercepts occur when \\(x = 0\\). Substitute \\(x = 0\\) into the equation and solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n0 = 2y^2 - 6y + 3.\n\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation of the form \\(2y^2 - 6y + 3 = 0\\). We can solve this using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 3\\):\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 24}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nSo, the \\(y\\)-intercepts are \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\). Therefore, \\(b = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(c = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nFinally, we need to find \\(a + b + c\\):\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 + \\frac{6}{2} = 3 + 3 = 6.\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}.\n\\]",
